{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:29:04.991Z","data":{"turn":60,"content":"{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01H2PbafMVKN4H3px7G2wgux\",\"name\":\"Write\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/repos/webvulnbench/deliverables/auth_exploitation_queue.json\",\"content\":\"{\\n  \\\"vulnerabilities\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-01\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Token_Management_Issue\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"ALL authenticated endpoints (JWT Bearer path)\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/config/index.js:30 — hardcoded fallback: `secret: process.env.JWT_SECRET || 'hireflow2024api'`; consumed by src/middleware/auth.js:25 and src/auth/auth.service.js:75\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"JWT signing secret is a hardcoded, publicly known string ('hireflow2024api'). No environment-specific secret rotation. Any actor with this value can forge a valid JWT for any user ID.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker signs a crafted JWT payload {id: <target_user_id>, email: <any>, role: 'superadmin', walletBalance: 0} using HS256 with secret 'hireflow2024api', then sends it as a Bearer token to any authenticated endpoint, successfully authenticating as the target user — including with superadmin privileges.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"jwt_secret_forgery\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Target user IDs are obtainable from public endpoints GET /api/users/:id and GET /api/users?search=. Admin/superadmin user IDs may be discoverable via GET /api/admin/users (once any valid auth is obtained). The authenticate middleware (auth.js:25-29) re-fetches the user from PostgreSQL by decoded.id, so the forged JWT only needs a valid user ID — the role claim in the JWT is ignored (DB role is used). However, a superadmin-role JWT for any valid user ID will pass all requireRole checks.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-02\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Session_Management_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"ALL authenticated endpoints (session cookie path)\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/config/index.js:25 — `secret: process.env.SESSION_SECRET || 'hireflow-session-key-change-in-production'`; applied at src/index.js:61-71 via express-session\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Express session cookie (connect.sid) is HMAC-signed with a publicly known hardcoded secret. Attacker can forge valid signed session cookie values without needing server access.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker uses the known session secret to forge a signed connect.sid cookie pointing to a session object injected into Redis (port 6379, no auth), then presents the forged cookie to the application and is authenticated as the target user.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_hijacking\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Redis is directly exposed on host port 6379 with no authentication. Session data format in Redis uses express-session connect-redis serialization. Combined with AUTH-VULN-01 (JWT forgery), this provides two independent full-auth-bypass vectors. Cookie flags: httpOnly=true, secure=false (non-production), no SameSite.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-03\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Abuse_Defenses_Missing\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/login\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.routes.js (no authLimiter import/usage); src/middleware/rateLimiter.js:13-19 (authLimiter defined but never applied to auth routes); src/index.js:74 (only apiLimiter applied globally)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"No per-endpoint or per-account rate limit on the login endpoint. The general apiLimiter allows 200 requests/15 min (1000 in dev). No account lockout, no exponential backoff, no CAPTCHA.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker performs a brute-force or credential-stuffing attack against POST /api/auth/login, submitting hundreds of password guesses per 15-minute window without being throttled or locked out, eventually authenticating as a valid user.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"brute_force_login\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Also applies to POST /api/auth/forgot-password and POST /api/auth/reset-password which have zero dedicated rate limiting. Combined with AUTH-VULN-06 (user enumeration via forgot-password), targeted per-account brute force is feasible.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-04\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Session_Management_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/logout\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.controller.js:111-124 — req.session.destroy() only; no JWT revocation; src/auth/auth.service.js:75 (7d JWT TTL, no jti/blacklist)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Logout invalidates only the server-side session. JWT tokens issued at login remain cryptographically valid for their full 7-day lifetime. No JWT blacklist, no jti tracking, no token family revocation.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker who captures a victim user's JWT Bearer token (via network interception, logs, or client-side theft) retains full authenticated access to all API endpoints for up to 7 days, even after the victim has explicitly logged out.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"token_replay\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"JWT payload: {id, email, role, walletBalance}. Signed HS256, 7d expiry. No refresh token mechanism. The session cookie path IS properly invalidated on logout, but the JWT path persists.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-05\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Login_Flow_Logic\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/login\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.controller.js:94-97 — `req.session.userId = user.id` without calling req.session.regenerate(); src/index.js:67-70 — no SameSite, secure=false\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"No session ID regeneration after successful authentication. The pre-login session ID is reused post-login, enabling session fixation attacks. Additionally, the session cookie lacks the `secure` flag (transmitted over HTTP) and has no `SameSite` attribute.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker establishes a known session ID by connecting to the application (receiving a connect.sid), then tricks a victim into authenticating while that session ID is active. After the victim logs in, the attacker uses the same session ID to access the victim's authenticated session.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_fixation\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"Medium\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Session fixation exploitability depends on whether the attacker can inject a known session ID into the victim's browser (requires XSS or HTTP header injection). The missing secure flag makes session cookies transmittable over HTTP, increasing MitM-based interception risk.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-06\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Login_Flow_Logic\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/forgot-password\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.controller.js:136-138 — `return res.status(404).json({ message: 'No account found with that email address' })`\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Distinct HTTP status code (404) and message returned when email is not registered, vs. 200 success when it is. Should return 200 with identical message regardless of account existence.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker submits a list of candidate email addresses to POST /api/auth/forgot-password and identifies which ones correspond to registered accounts by observing 404 vs. 200 responses, building a valid target list for credential attacks.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"account_enumeration\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"No rate limiting on this endpoint compounds the risk — enumeration can proceed freely. Enumerated emails can then be used for targeted brute-force (AUTH-VULN-03) or phishing.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-07\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/forgot-password + POST /api/auth/reset-password\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/utils/helpers.js:25-29 — generateResetToken(): `const timestamp = Date.now().toString(36); const hash = crypto.createHash('sha256').update(email + timestamp).digest('hex').slice(0, 16); return timestamp + '-' + hash`\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Reset token is deterministic (derived from email + timestamp) rather than cryptographically random. Timestamp is publicly encoded in the token itself. Hash component is only 16 hex chars (8 bytes). NIST SP 800-63B requires at least 20 bytes of cryptographic randomness.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker targeting a known user email requests a password reset, extracts the timestamp component from the token format (readable directly from the MailHog web UI at port 8025, which captures all emails with no authentication), and uses the token directly from MailHog to complete an account takeover reset before the legitimate user acts.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"reset_token_guessing\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"MailHog (port 8025) is publicly accessible with no authentication and captures ALL outbound emails including reset links. The DB expiry field (reset_token_expires) is stored but NEVER queried in findByResetToken() — expiry is checked only via the embedded timestamp extracted client-side in the controller.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-08\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/reset-password\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.service.js:128-135 — updatePassword() only updates password_hash and updated_at; does NOT clear reset_token or reset_token_expires columns\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Used reset tokens are not cleared from the database after a successful password reset. The token remains valid and reusable for up to 24 hours after the legitimate reset operation.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker who observes a password reset token (e.g., from MailHog, HTTP interception, or email logs) after the legitimate user has already used it can replay the same token to POST /api/auth/reset-password within the 24-hour window and set a new password, completing an account takeover.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"reset_token_guessing\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"The 24-hour expiry window is checked in the controller by extracting the timestamp from the token string (tokenAge > 86400000). The DB column reset_token_expires is stored but never used in any query. Single-use enforcement is completely absent.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-09\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Transport_Exposure\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/login, POST /api/auth/register, POST /api/auth/forgot-password, POST /api/auth/reset-password\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"Infrastructure: Nginx config (HTTP only, port 80); Express direct on port 3000 (HTTP only); src/index.js:67-70 (secure: config.env === 'production' — false in deployment)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"No TLS/HTTPS configured anywhere in the stack. No HSTS header. No HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect. Session cookie secure flag is false (conditional on NODE_ENV=production which is not set). All credentials, session cookies, JWT tokens, and reset links are transmitted in cleartext.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"A network-level attacker (on the same network segment, or via ARP spoofing/rogue AP) passively intercepts POST /api/auth/login requests and extracts email/password credentials in cleartext, then uses them to authenticate as the victim.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_hijacking\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Both Nginx (port 80) and direct Express (port 3000) are HTTP only. The secure cookie flag is gated on NODE_ENV=production which is not set in the observed deployment.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-10\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Abuse_Defenses_Missing\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/register, POST /api/auth/login\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.service.js:8 — `const SALT_ROUNDS = 4`; applied at createUser() line 11 and updatePassword() line 129\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"bcrypt work factor of 4 is far below the recommended minimum of 10-12. Passwords are approximately 64x easier to crack offline compared to 10-round bcrypt. No password complexity requirements beyond 8-character length.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker who obtains a dump of the users table (via SQL injection sinks at GET /api/users?search= or direct PostgreSQL access on port 5432) performs offline dictionary/brute-force attacks against the bcrypt hashes at 4 rounds, recovering plaintext passwords at a significantly accelerated rate.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"credential_stuffing\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Requires prior DB exfiltration (via SQLi or direct PostgreSQL port 5432 access). Work factor 4 means approximately 16 iterations per hash instead of 1024 (round 10) — roughly 64x faster cracking. Combined with minimum-8-char-only policy, weak or common passwords will crack quickly.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-11\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Authentication_Bypass\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"WebSocket ws://host.docker.internal:3000/socket.io\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/config/socket.js:18 — `const userId = socket.handshake.query.userId;` — no session or JWT validation performed\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Socket.IO default namespace accepts user identity (userId) directly from the client-supplied query parameter with no server-side verification against session or JWT. Any actor can claim to be any user.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker connects to the Socket.IO endpoint with ?userId=<target_user_id> and is registered as that user in the server-side onlineUsers map. The server broadcasts user:online events attributing the attacker's connection to the target user, and the attacker can join any conversation room via join:conversation events, enabling real-time eavesdropping on messaging activity.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_fixation\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"User IDs are obtainable from public API endpoints. Socket.IO CORS policy is origin='*' (wildcard), allowing connections from any domain. The typing event also accepts userId from the client payload (socket.js:35-37), allowing spoofed typing indicators attributed to arbitrary users.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:29:04.991Z"}}
